  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-2x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$\\,{x}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-2x} & \u27f9 &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-2x)^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{8}{(1-2x)^{3}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+2x+\\frac{8}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2\\,x+4\\,{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
